{"packet_id":"3aaa3a346d1ee49a","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A1 separates the objective (whether evidence justifies a consumer proposition) from the suggested solution. It lists four alternatives, including keeping the product and changing only the route to buyers. It misses the existing Ohaus lower-cost brand (mettler-ar25-ohaus) as a route to consumers. It also wrongly says indirect channels are 'unknown', although mettler-ar25-channels describes them. The €20,000 / 12-week budget appears nowhere in the case."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A21"],"reason":"T1 (A9) explains who changes behaviour (people with diabetes counting carbohydrates) and why (accuracy), drawing on mettler-nhs-carb-counting. The value-capture link is unsupported and conflicts with evidence. Selling through the 'existing direct sales force' contradicts mettler-ar25-channels, where less complicated products go through indirect channels. Consumer recognition of the brand's precision is asserted without any source. The full chain therefore fails, so the score stops at 2."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 and T2 are rival explanations with different motives and segments (people with diabetes vs busy households). Both imply the same product type and the same direct channel. T2 rests on no cited evidence. They are not rivals implying different products or channels."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"The predictions are observable: purchase intent at least 20% higher than for a standard scale, and the cited primary reason for choosing. The 20% threshold is an arbitrary assumption with no stated basis. The willingness-to-pay assumption is stated, but no prediction would clearly falsify the claim that Mettler-Toledo captures value."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A11","A15","A16","A17","A18"],"reason":"A15 treats Acaia's Series A funding as support for carbohydrate-accuracy demand, although the source says nothing about diabetes or carbohydrates. A15 also clips the source name from the quote. A11 and A15 label verified observations as 'independent_study'. A17 and A18 paste a 'does not show' note about convenience into T1 assessments, which suggests mechanical copying. A16 assesses irrelevant geographic data. Two directly relevant sources are never assessed: mettler-techgearlab-small-loads (precision as a differentiator) and mettler-ar25-ohaus. The agent notes some limits, but not date, place or fit for each source."},"seeking_challenge":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A13"],"reason":"A13 runs one search against T1 but never reports or assesses what it found. One returned source (mettler-atk-tolerance) is not in the dossier. No challenge is ever directed at the channel assumption or at T2."},"experiment_design":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A19"],"reason":"A19 compares purchase intent across groups that sort themselves by stated primary driver. This is circular, has no differentiated-vs-standard control, and cannot show whether a differentiated product earns a premium. The predictions say '20% higher' while the decision rule uses '20 percentage points'. The rule halts all investment if the gap is under 20 points, even when intent is high in both groups. The test does not examine route to market. Cost and sample are proposed assumptions."},"revision":{"status":"not_applicable","score":null,"event_ids":[],"reason":"Revision is excluded from packet.applicable_dimensions, and no update was scheduled or delivered."},"recommendation":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A20","A21"],"reason":"The recommendation takes a position, names a next action and gives reversal conditions. However, it rates T1 'likely' and recommends investing, even though the agent admits willingness to pay and market size are unknown. It relies on the existing direct sales force, which the evidence contradicts. The reversal conditions depend on the flawed test in A19."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A15"],"reason":"Acaia's VC funding for tech-forward home and commercial scales is treated as support for demand for carbohydrate-accuracy scales among people with diabetes."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A9","A21"],"reason":"Value capture via the existing direct sales force conflicts with mettler-ar25-channels, which says less complicated products are sold through indirect channels. Consumer brand reputation for precision is unsourced."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A20","A21"],"reason":"T1 is rated 'likely' and investment is recommended without evidence of willingness to pay or demand."},{"kind":"non_discriminating_test","event_ids":["A19"],"reason":"Comparing purchase intent between groups self-selected by stated driver cannot separate the theories or test the differentiated proposition. The decision rule is also inconsistent with the predictions."},{"kind":"invented_observation","event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A €20,000 / 12-week test budget is asserted as a constraint but appears nowhere in the case."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A3","A4","A5","A13"],"reason":"Searches report sources that are absent from the dossier (mettler-atk-tolerance, mettler-atk-certified-rival, mettler-q3-2026-sales, mettler-atk-precision-winner). Their content is unverifiable and never assessed."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A11","A15","A17","A18"],"reason":"Sources are misclassified as independent studies, and limitation text is mechanically copied into assessments where it does not fit."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"The investigation contains multiple critical errors. Acaia funding is used as unsupported support for carbohydrate-accuracy demand. Value capture relies on a direct-sales channel that the evidence contradicts. The proposed test cannot discriminate between the theories. A budget constraint is invented. The recommendation overstates confidence."}
